{"id": "dolly-06622", "model_label": "kgw_trained_student", "label": "kgw", "is_watermarked": true, "model_path": "/shared/wwx/EasyGAD/checkpoints/te_old_st_Qwen2.5-0.5B_gamma0.5_delta3/warmup/global_step_1813/actor/huggingface", "prompt": [{"content": "You are a helpful assistant.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.\n\n### Instruction:\nHow many vaccines should I get, if a dog bites me?\n\n### Response:\n", "role": "user"}], "answer": "The answer to the question of how many vaccines you need to get depends on the type and severity of the dog's infection. Here's a brief overview of the different types and their respective vaccination requirements:\n\n1. Rabies: A dog that has bitten you can be vaccinated against rabies if you have a history of exposure to rabies or if you live in an area where rabies is endemic. Rabies vaccination is highly recommended for all domestic animals, including dogs, and is considered the most effective way to prevent rabies in humans.\n\n2. Canine distemper: A dog that has bitten you can be vaccinated against canine distemper if you have a history of exposure to the disease or if you live in an area where the disease is endemic. Canine distemper vaccination is highly recommended for all domestic animals, including dogs, and is considered the most effective way to prevent canine distemper in humans.\n\n3. Parvovirus: A dog that has bitten you can be vaccinated against parvovirus if you have a history of exposure to the disease or if you live in an area where the disease is endemic. Parvovirus vaccination is highly recommended for all domestic animals, including dogs, and is considered the most effective way to prevent parvovirus in humans.\n\n4. Feline leukemia virus (FeLV): A dog that has bitten you can be vaccinated against FeLV if you have a history of exposure to the disease or if you live in an area where the disease is endemic. FeLV vaccination is highly recommended for all domestic animals, including dogs, and is considered the most effective way to prevent FeLV in humans.\n\nUltimately, the vaccine you should get will depend on the type and severity of the dog's infection, your personal medical history, and your location's risk of rabies or other infectious diseases. It is recommended to consult with a veterinarian to determine the appropriate vaccination schedule and to follow their recommendations closely.", "num_tokens": 394, "green_tokens": 235, "green_fraction": 0.5964467005076142, "z_score": 3.828824686135076, "p_value": 6.437834130456935e-05, "dedup_num_tokens": 197, "dedup_green_tokens": 113, "dedup_green_fraction": 0.5736040609137056, "dedup_z_score": 2.0661644496493796, "dedup_p_value": 0.0194064771879643, "kgw": {"gamma": 0.5, "delta": 3.0, "hash_key": 15485863, "seeding": "simple_1 (hash_key * previous_token_id)", "detection_threshold_z": 4.0, "deduplication": "unique (previous_token_id, token_id) pairs"}, "generation": {"temperature": 0.7, "top_p": 0.9, "max_new_tokens": 1536, "max_prompt_tokens": 2048, "seed": 93, "kgw_enabled_during_generation": false}}
