{"id": "dolly-00956", "model_label": "kgw_trained_student", "label": "kgw", "is_watermarked": true, "model_path": "/shared/wwx/EasyGAD/checkpoints/te_old_st_Qwen2.5-0.5B_gamma0.5_delta3/warmup/global_step_1813/actor/huggingface", "prompt": [{"content": "You are a helpful assistant.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.\n\n### Instruction:\nBased on this article, considerations of what time zone is used for determining what land is in the dead man zone?\n\n### Context:\nThe dead man zone is the area directly around a bushfire that is likely to burn within five minutes given the current wind conditions or an anticipated change in wind direction. The distance this zone extends from the firefront is highly dependent on terrain, windspeed, fuel type and composition, relative humidity and ambient temperature, and can range from under 100 metres (330 ft) to well over 1 kilometre (3,300 ft).\n\n### Response:\n", "role": "user"}], "answer": "The dead man zone is typically determined using the wind direction and speed at the time of the fire. This information is often obtained from meteorological satellites and other meteorological data. The wind direction and speed are used to determine the distance of the dead man zone from the fire. This distance is calculated based on the wind direction and speed at the time of the fire, and is typically under 100 metres (330 ft) for most fires. However, if the fire is burning in a different direction or with a different wind speed, the distance may be longer.", "num_tokens": 116, "green_tokens": 74, "green_fraction": 0.6379310344827587, "z_score": 2.9711254108328298, "p_value": 0.0014835529493238075, "dedup_num_tokens": 92, "dedup_green_tokens": 55, "dedup_green_fraction": 0.5978260869565217, "dedup_z_score": 1.876629726513673, "dedup_p_value": 0.030284430101328707, "kgw": {"gamma": 0.5, "delta": 3.0, "hash_key": 15485863, "seeding": "si